</style></head><body><header><a href="/">Game AI Checker</a><a href="/browse/">Browse game evidence →</a></header><main><p class="muted">PRACTICAL GUIDE · Updated October 27, 2026</p><h1>How to check if a Steam game uses AI</h1><div class="answer"><b>Open the game's Steam store page and find “AI Generated Content Disclosure.”</b><p>Read the developer's full wording, then check dated developer statements and discussion sources. An absent disclosure does not prove that a game is AI-free.</p><a class="cta" href="/">Search a game now →</a></div><nav class="steps" aria-label="Guide steps"><a href="#steam"><b>1 · Find the disclosure</b>Check the correct Steam App ID.</a><a href="#steamdb"><b>2 · Use SteamDB</b>Discover games with a disclosure.</a><a href="#evidence"><b>3 · Read the evidence</b>Separate claims from confirmation.</a></nav>
<section id="steam"><h2>1. Where is the AI disclosure on Steam?</h2><ol><li>Open the store page for the full game. A demo has its own App ID and may have different wording.</li><li>Scroll through the description area, or use your browser's Find command for <b>AI Generated Content Disclosure</b> on the English page.</li><li>Read the entire statement beneath that heading. Record the store URL and the date you checked.</li></ol><p>If it does not appear, verify that the complete page loaded. An age gate, region restriction or failed request is an incomplete check.</p><details><summary>What does Steam ask developers to disclose?</summary><p>Valve's <a href="https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/contentsurvey" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Content Survey documentation</a> distinguishes content generated before release from content generated while the game runs. It also describes additional requirements for live generation. Read the current rules directly; a store disclosure is a developer statement about content, not an audit of every production tool.</p></details></section>
<section id="steamdb"><h2>2. Can SteamDB filter games that disclose AI?</h2><p>Yes. Open SteamDB's <a href="https://steamdb.info/tag/1368160/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Content Disclosed listing</a>. Use its product-type and other filters to narrow the results, then open each game's actual Steam page for the wording.</p><p>The filter is useful for discovery. It does not tell you which of the eight areas used AI, whether the content was later replaced, or whether a missing label means no AI use.</p></section>
<section id="evidence"><h2>3. What can each source establish?</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>What you find</th><th>Useful conclusion</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Steam AI disclosure</td><td>The developer discloses the stated use.</td><td>Do not infer other asset types.</td></tr><tr><td>Dated developer statement</td><td>A claim about the areas it explicitly names.</td><td>Self-report, not independent certification.</td></tr><tr><td>Media report or interview</td><td>Attributed reporting and context.</td><td>Read the original source; check its date and quotes.</td></tr><tr><td>Player allegation</td><td>A question or allegation worth investigating.</td><td>Appearance alone does not prove AI generation.</td></tr><tr><td>No disclosure found</td><td>No disclosure visible in the checked page.</td><td>Does not establish zero AI use.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
<section><h2>Check the scope, not just “yes” or “no”</h2><p>Look separately at in-game generation, code, art, music, voice, writing, translation and marketing. A statement about one area does not settle the others.</p><details><summary>Does “AI” mean enemy behavior or generated content?</summary><p>Traditional game AI, such as pathfinding and enemy decision rules, is different from generative AI used to create assets or content. A game about fighting an AI character does not, by itself, disclose generative-AI production.</p></details><details><summary>What if the disclosure only says “some assets”?</summary><p>Record the wording and leave the area unspecified. Do not turn it into a claim about art, music or voice without an explicit source.</p></details><details><summary>What if AI content was removed?</summary><p>Keep the older statement and the dated update distinct. “Previously used and later replaced” is different from “never used.” A current store statement can lag behind a production change.</p></details></section>
<section><h2>Examples with original sources</h2><ul><li><a href="/games/5012840-tooth-for-tooth/">Tooth for Tooth</a> — read the disclosure and the area-by-area record.</li><li><a href="/games/2456740-inzoi/">inZOI</a> — distinguish in-game generation from pre-generated assets.</li><li><a href="/games/4019220-dressmaker/">Dressmaker</a> — check the scope and review status of a human-made claim.</li></ul><p><a href="/events/steam-next-fest-october-2026-ai-disclosures/">Browse reviewed Next Fest games with AI disclosures →</a></p></section>
<section><h2>A quick evidence checklist</h2><ul><li>Same App ID, developer and full game versus demo.</li><li>Full original wording and date checked.</li><li>Explicitly named areas only.</li><li>Player allegations labeled separately.</li><li>Later corrections or replacements included.</li></ul><a class="cta" href="/">Check a game in Game AI Checker →</a><p class="muted">Our records link to original sources and record review limits. They are not automated visual AI detection.</p></section></main><footer>Sources: <a href="https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/contentsurvey">Valve Content Survey</a> · <a href="https://steamdb.info/tag/1368160/">SteamDB AI Content Disclosed</a> · <a href="/methodology/">Our methodology</a></footer></body></html>
